          %h1#koszul_duality Koszul duality
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#on_operads"} On operads
              %li
                %a{:href => "#for_associative_and_dgalgebras"} For associative and dg-algebras
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#koszul_duality_between_dmodules_and_modules"} Koszul duality between D-modules and Ω-modules
              %li
                %a{:href => "#linear_koszul_duality"} Linear Koszul duality
              %li
                %a{:href => "#categorical_koszul_duality"} Categorical Koszul duality
              %li
                %a{:href => "#global_koszul_duality"} Global Koszul duality
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %strong Koszul duality
            (named after
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Louis+Koszul"} Jean-Louis Koszul
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+graded+algebra"} differential graded algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisting+cochain"} twisting cochain
            (see that entry).
          %h2#on_operads On operads
          %p
            Koszul duality is a duality between
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quadratic+operad"} quadratic operad
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"}> M. Kontsevich
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Formal (non)commutative symplectic geometry
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+noncommutative+symplectic+geometry"} formal noncommutative symplectic geometry
          %p and developed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Victor+Ginzburg"}> Victor Ginzburg
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mikhail+Kapranov"} Mikhail Kapranov
              = succeed "," do
                %em Koszul duality for operads
              %strong 76
              (1994), no. 1, 203–272; reprint
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0709.1228"} arxiv/0709.1228
              = succeed "," do
                %em Erratum to: Koszul duality for operads
              %strong 80
              (1995), no. 1, 293.
          %h2#for_associative_and_dgalgebras For associative and dg-algebras
          %p
            From Ben Webster on
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "https://mathoverflow.net/a/394/2362"} mathoverflow
          %p
            There are a lot of algebras whose derived categories are equivalent in surprising ways.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+equivalences"} Morita equivalences
            are pretty simple, especially for finite dimensional algebras; essentially the only free parameter is the dimension of the object.
          %p
            Namely, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mi Mod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A Mod
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mi Mod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B Mod
            are equivalent, then the image of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            as a module over itself is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+object"} projective
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generator"} generator
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mi Mod
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B Mod
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/indecomposable+object"} indecomposible
            projectives of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          %p
            On the other hand, if you take the derived category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+graded+module"} dg-modules
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            (the dg part of this is not a huge deal; it’s just that they’re very close to, but a bit better behaved than, actual derived/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulated+categories"}> triangulated categories
            , which are just crude truncations of truly functorial dg/
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\infty
            versions), this is equivalent to the category of dg-modules over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endomorphism+algebra"} endomorphism algebra
            (this is in the dg sense, so it’s a dg-algebra whose cohomology is the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ext
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ext
            algebra) of
            %em any
            generating object. There are a
            %em lot
            more generating objects than projective generators, so there are a lot of derived equivalences.
          %p
            In particular, let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            be a finite dimensional algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+modules"} simple modules
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mi dg
                    %mi Mod
                    %mo =
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Ext
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo ,
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi dg
                    %mi Mod
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A dg Mod = \mathrm{Ext}(L,L) dg Mod
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Ext
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Ext}(L,-)
          %p
            In general,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Ext
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo ,
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Ext}(L,L)
            is a very complicated object (for example, often for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+algebras"} group algebras
            over
            = succeed "s)," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+field"} finite field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exterior+algebra"} exterior algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial+ring"} polynomial ring
            on the dual vector space. Another (closely related) example is that the cohomology of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reductive+group"} reductive group
            (over
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
          %p
            One way to ensure that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Ext
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo ,
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Ext}(L,L)
            is nice is if the algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+algebra"} graded
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Ext
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo ,
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Ext}(L,L)
            inherits an “internal” grading in addition to its homological one. If these coincide, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is called
            = succeed "." do
              %em Koszul
          %p
            In this case,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo =
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Ext
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo ,
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B=\mathrm{Ext}(L,L)
            is forced to be formal (if it had any interesting
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\infty
            operations, they would break the grading), so you’re dealing with a derived equivalence between actual algebras, though you have to be a bit careful about the dg-issues. Thus the derived category of usual modules over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is equivalent to dg-modules over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            (with its unique grading) and vice versa. This can be fixed by taking graded modules on both sides.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The most famous example of Koszul dual algebras are the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exterior+algebra"} exterior algebra
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Alt
                        %mo •
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Alt^{\bullet}V[-1]
                and the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial+algebra"} polynomial algebra
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Sym
                          %mo •
                        %msup
                          %mi V
                          %mo *
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sym^\bullet V^*[-2]
            %li
              %p
                A regular block of
                %span.newWikiWord
                  category O
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/category+O"} ?
                for any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semisimple+Lie+algebra"} semisimple Lie algebra
                is a self-Koszul dual.
              %ul
                %li
                  More generally, a singular block of
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    parabolic category O
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/parabolic+category+O"} ?
                  is dual to a different singular block of parabolic category O where the combinatorial data determining the central character and finiteness conditions switch.
            %li
              %p
                Braden, Licata, Proudfoot and Webster gave a combinatorial construction of a large family of Koszul dual algebras in
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0806.3256"} Gale duality and Koszul duality
          %h2#koszul_duality_between_dmodules_and_modules Koszul duality between D-modules and Ω-modules
          %p An important special case of Koszul duality establishes a Quillen equivalence between model categories of D-modules and Ω-modules. This was first observed by Kapranov.
          %p
            Here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is the sheaf of differential operators on a smooth manifold or a smooth variety and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            is the sheaf of differential forms on the same manifold or variety. Both
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            are equipped with their canonical filtrations (differential operators of order at most~
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            respectively differential forms of degree at least~
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            ) and all constructions below work with sheaves of filtered chain complexes.
          %p
            The equivalence is implemented by tensoring with a certain filtered
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            = succeed "-bimodule" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi DR
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DR
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi DR
                  %mo =
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DR=\Omega\otimes D
            as a filtered graded bimodule. The differential is canonically determined by its degree 0 component, where we take the coevaluation map
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi O
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msubsup
                      %mi D
                      %mn 0
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O\to \Omega^1\otimes D^1_0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi D
                    %mn 0
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^1_0
            denotes differential operators of order at most~1 with vanishing constant term, i.e., vector fields.
          %p
            This equivalence allows one to define
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/six+operations"} six operations
            on D-modules by transfering them from Ω-modules, where they can be defined in the usual manner, since differential forms can be pulled back along maps, unlike differential operators.
          %p
            This fully explains the somewhat unintuitive explicit formulas for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/six+operations"} six operations
            on D-modules.
          %h2#linear_koszul_duality Linear Koszul duality
          %p Linear Koszul duality is an equivalence between the symmetric algebra of a dg vector bundle and the symmetric algebra of the shifted dual dg vector bundle.
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ivan+Mirkovi%C4%87"}> Ivan Mirković
              , Simon Riche,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Linear Koszul duality
              = succeed ":1" do
                %strong 146
          %h2#categorical_koszul_duality Categorical Koszul duality
          %p
            A form of Koszul duality with dg algebras replaced by dg categories has been developed by Holstein and Lazarev. In this, the Koszul dual of a dg category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mi 𝒟
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B\mathcal{D}
            is a pointed curved coalgebra; see
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Julian+Holstein"}> Julian Holstein
              , A. Lazarev,
              %em Categorical Koszul duality
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2006.01705.pdf"} arXiv:2006.01706
          %h2#global_koszul_duality Global Koszul duality
          %p Another variant of Koszul duality defined in terms of model structures on curved dg coalgebras.
          %ul
            %li
              Matt Booth, Andrey Lazarev,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Global Koszul duality
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.08409"} arXiv:2304.08409
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bar+and+cobar+construction"} bar and cobar construction
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holography+as+Koszul+duality"} holography as Koszul duality
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+duality"} symplectic duality
          %h2#references References
          %p Other historical references on Koszul duality include
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A. A. Beilinson, V. A. Ginsburg, V. V. Schechtman,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Koszul duality
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=1048505"} MR1048505
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/0393-0440(88)90028-9"} doi:10.1016/0393-0440(88)90028-9
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Beilinson"}> Alexander Beilinson
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Victor+Ginzburg"} Victor Ginzburg
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wolfgang+Soergel"} Wolfgang Soergel
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Koszul duality patterns in representation theory
                %strong 9
                2 (1996) 473-527 &amp;lbrack;corrigenda
                %a{:href => "http://home.mathematik.uni-freiburg.de/soergel/PReprints/KorrBGS.pdf"}> pdf
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p Koszul duality is also discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://math.ucr.edu/home/baez/week238.html"} TWF Week 238
                %a{:href => "http://math.ucr.edu/home/baez/week239.html"} Week 239
            %li
              %p
                %em The Everything Seminar
                ,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://cornellmath.wordpress.com/2008/03/25/koszul-duality-and-lie-algebroids"} koszul-duality-and-lie-algebroids
            %li
              %p
                MathOverflow:
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/329/what-is-koszul-duality"} What is Koszul duality?
            %li
